<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/intermediate/charts-that-tell-the-truth">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Visualisation is <strong>argument by encoding</strong>: whatever you map to position,
    length, area and colour becomes the claim the audience absorbs instantly, before
    reading a single word. A chart is not an illustration of an argument — it <em>is</em>
    the argument, in the audience's visual system, where it lands faster and sticks
    harder than prose. Honest charts align the encoding with the data's importance and
    structure; dishonest ones (truncated axes, cherry-picked windows, dual scales)
    mislead without a single false word — which is why chart literacy is an ethics
    skill as much as a design one.
  </p>
  <p>
    This lesson pairs the EDA lesson (which charts for <em>yourself</em>, to see) with
    the craft of charting for <em>others</em> — where the audience's perception, not
    your own exploration speed, is the constraint — and it ends each section with the
    failure mode, because visual honesty is learned mostly by studying how charts lie.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four rules of charts that tell the truth" widget="equationStep" config={chartsCfg} />

  <h2>The perceptual hierarchy: what eyes judge well</h2>
  <p>
    The scientific foundation is Cleveland and McGill's experiments (published in
    Science, 1984): people judge <strong>position along a common scale</strong> most
    accurately, <strong>length</strong> nearly as well; <strong>slopes and angles</strong>
    poorly; <strong>area</strong> worse still; and <strong>colour saturation and hue</strong>
    worst of all. The design consequence is a hierarchy, not a preference: your most
    important variable belongs on an axis (scatter plots, line charts), the second in
    length (bars), and area and colour should carry only rough, secondary
    distinctions. This single result organises most chart advice. It is why the
    scatter plot beats the bubble chart, why the grouped bar beats the pie, and why
    "which slice is bigger?" is a question pie charts answer badly (angles) that a
    sorted bar chart answers instantly (length). Pie charts survive for exactly one
    job — a part-to-whole with very few slices where the 50% comparison matters —
    and even then, a stacked single bar does the same job with length instead of
    angle.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing the encoding by the question</h2>
  <p>
    Chart type follows question type, mechanically. <strong>Comparison across
    categories</strong> → bar chart, sorted by value (alphabetical sorting wastes the
    encoding: the reader's first question is "which is biggest?", and the sort should
    answer it before the labels do). <strong>Trend over time</strong> → line chart
    (position on a continuous axis; time goes left to right, never chopped into
    unlabeled segments). <strong>Relationship between two quantities</strong> →
    scatter plot. <strong>Distribution</strong> → histogram or boxplot (the EDA
    lesson's choices). <strong>Part-to-whole</strong> → stacked bars, or a 100% bar.
    <strong>Two-to-three-dimensional density</strong> → heatmap, with a sequential
    colour scale. The failure mode is decoration-driven selection: choosing by what
    looks impressive rather than by what question the reader is asking. A chart
    that answers no question is decoration, and decoration with numbers is worse
    than none — it borrows the authority of data for a shape that carries none.
  </p>

  <h2>The truncated axis: the most common lie</h2>
  <p>
    Bars encode value as <strong>length</strong>, so a bar chart that starts its
    y-axis anywhere but zero breaks its own encoding — the classic media sin. A
    2% difference rendered from a 90-starting axis looks like a cliff; the same
    data from zero looks like what it is, a rounding error. The rule is strict
    for bars and (by the same perception logic) for any filled shape. Line charts
    are different: they encode by <em>position</em>, so a non-zero baseline is
    legitimate when the interesting variation is small relative to the values —
    a stock index from zero hides the week's drama — but the truncation must be
    visible (axis labels that start at 4,000, not a cropped image that implies
    it). Tufte quantified the whole family of distortions with the
    <strong>lie factor</strong>: the size of the effect shown in the graphic
    divided by the size of the effect in the data. His famous example — a
    government fuel-economy chart whose drawn increase was nearly ten times the
    actual mpg increase — scored 9.4. The measure matters because it removes
    intent from the question: a misleading chart is misleading whether or not
    anyone meant it.
  </p>

  <h2>Dual axes, cherry-picked windows and other quieter lies</h2>
  <p>
    The subtler deceptions are structural. <strong>Dual axes</strong>: two series
    on two independent scales can be slid into any apparent relationship — the
    spurious-correlation factory; the honest use demands loud scale labelling and
    a claim that survives when either axis is rescaled (if the "correlation"
    changes with your zoom, the chart was the correlation). <strong>Window
    selection</strong>: a line that starts at the all-time peak tells a
    different story than the same line from the trough — start points are
    choices and should be defensible on the data's own terms, not the argument's.
    <strong>Aggregation level</strong>: daily data smoothed to quarters can erase
    (or manufacture) a trend; the honest version shows the native frequency or
    says what was smoothed. <strong>Inverted or nonlinear scales without
    disclosure</strong> — the notorious case of a chart whose y-axis ran
    downward, flipping a rise into a fall while every number on it stayed
    technically true. The unifying diagnosis: all of these encode a claim the
    data does not make. The defence is a habit — redraw every important chart
    you are shown with honest axes and a neutral window before accepting its
    story. Ten minutes of re-plotting is the fact-checking of the visual age.
  </p>

  <h2>Colour, order and the craft of attention</h2>
  <p>
    Colour is information and should be spent carefully. The working rule:
    <strong>one accent colour for the thing you are talking about, grey for
    everything else</strong> — the reader's eye goes exactly where the colour
    says the story is, and the chart now has a point of view it can defend.
    Sequence and order carry information too: sort bars by value (already
    argued), keep time on one axis only, and order stacked segments consistently
    across a series of charts so the reader's eye can track them. The palette
    failure modes are known: <strong>rainbow scales</strong> create fake
    boundaries and perceptual cliffs where the data is smooth (Alberto Cairo's
    chartjunk critique); <strong>red-green defaults</strong> fail colourblind
    readers — roughly one in twelve men — so use colourblind-safe sequential
    palettes (viridis and relatives) and never encode meaning in hue alone.
    And declutter deliberately: gridlines, borders and legends that do not
    answer questions are ink the reader pays attention tax on. Tufte's
    "data-ink ratio" — the share of ink devoted to data rather than
    furniture — is the pole star, applied with judgment rather than
    Puritanism.
  </p>

  <h2>Annotation and titles: the chart as sentence</h2>
  <p>
    A chart without explanation makes the reader write the explanation — and
    readers write the story their priors suggest, not yours. So supply it.
    <strong>Titles state findings</strong>: "Region C drives the decline" beats
    "Sales by region" because the reader arrives already pointed at the thing
    the chart shows; the title is a conclusion, not a caption (and if you cannot
    state a finding, the chart may not have one — which is also worth knowing
    before you present it). <strong>Annotate causes</strong>: mark the launch,
    the price change, the outage, the policy date directly on the line — the
    spike that needs a footnote becomes the spike with a label, and the
    explanation is now part of the artefact, challengeable and documented.
    <strong>Label directly</strong>: put values on the bars that matter instead
    of sending the eye to a legend and back. And <strong>show the uncertainty</strong>:
    error bars, confidence bands, or a plain "based on n = 40" note — a confident
    line without an interval is a claim disguised as a chart, and the audience
    deserves to know how much of the shape is signal.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked redesign: from misleading to defensible</h2>
  <p>
    Watch a bad chart become an honest one, because the diff is the lesson. The
    incoming slide: a bar chart of four regions' revenue growth, y-axis from 3.8 to
    4.2 (%), rainbow-coloured, alphabetical order, title "Revenue Growth by Region".
    Diagnosis, rule by rule: the baseline truncates bar length (a 0.1-point gap
    renders as a 25% height difference — a lie factor around 40); colour assigns
    rainbow urgency to four equal-status values; alphabetical order buries the
    ranking; the title promises data and delivers no claim. The redesign: horizontal
    bars from zero, sorted by value, one accent colour on the largest, grey on the
    rest, direct value labels, and a title that states what the data actually shows —
    "Growth is flat across regions (3.9–4.1%)" — which, notably, is the opposite of
    the original chart's visual story. That inversion is the point: truncation did
    not just exaggerate, it manufactured drama that did not exist. The honest chart
    is less exciting and more true — and when a stakeholder asks "why does the new
    chart look so boring?", you have the definition of the job: the drama should be
    in the data, or it should not be in the chart.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading charts critically: the audience's side</h2>
  <p>
    The same machinery, reversed, is a defence skill — most charts you will meet
    were made by someone with an incentive. The five-question audit takes a
    minute: <strong>where does the axis start</strong> (and would the story
    survive a zero baseline)? <strong>where does the window start and end</strong>
    (and why those dates)? <strong>what is encoded in colour and size</strong>
    (and does the most dramatic encoding carry the most reliable data)?
    <strong>what is the denominator</strong> (percent of what — the base of a
    percentage is where most chart tricks actually live)? <strong>what is
    missing</strong> (error bars, sample sizes, the segments that were dropped)?
    Run the audit on the next compelling chart you see in the wild — a news
    graphic, a pitch deck — and note how often the story changes under the
    questions. Chart literacy in this sense is the visual sibling of the
    statistics ladder's source-skepticism: the encoding is the claim, and the
    claim deserves to be read.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four expert directions. <strong>Small multiples</strong> (Tufte again): the
    same small chart repeated per segment — one panel per region, identical
    scales — beats every attempt to cram segments into one frame; comparison
    across identical little charts is what human vision does best. <strong>The
    animation trap</strong>: animated charts (racing bars) are engaging and
    treacherous — motion hides the axis decisions, and the audience remembers
    the drama rather than the values; if you animate, freeze frames of the
    decision moments. <strong>Dashboards versus charts</strong>: a dashboard is
    an instrument panel (monitored repeatedly, needs glancability) while an
    explanatory chart makes one argument once — the most common professional
    failure is presenting instrument panels when the meeting needed sentences,
    and vice versa. <strong>The honest-chart checklist</strong> worth
    internalising as a final gate: baseline defensible? windows defensible?
    encodings match the data's precision? uncertainty visible? colour spent on
    the story? title a claim you can defend? A chart that passes its own
    interrogation is not just honest — it is <em>stronger</em>, because every
    challenge you pre-empted is one the audience doesn't get to win.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Tufte, The Visual Display of Quantitative Information (1983; 2nd ed. 2001); Cleveland &amp; McGill, Science (1984); Cairo, The Truthful Art (2016); Few, Information Dashboard Design.</p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: charts and their honesty" widget="quiz" config={chartsQuizCfg} />
</TopicPage>
